    "title": "Coborn's plans a second Market & Table store in the Twin Cities - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Brianna Kelly ,\n10 hours ago\nCoborn’s is duplicating its new grocery store concept, highlighting local products and chefs within a sprawling mixed-use development in downtown Chanhassen.\nThe second Coborn’s Market & Table location is planned as part of the Avienda project that’s currently underway on a 118-acre site near Highway 212 and Powers Boulevard.\nLike the prototype in Plymouth that debuted in March , the full-service grocery store will have a dining area with made-to-order and prepackaged food from The Global Table by chef David Fhima and “Cupcake Wars” winner Alicia Hinze’s The Buttered Tin, plus an old-fashioned ice cream parlor and a liquor store.\nThere will also be a patio with a small stage, an outdoor playground, and a gas station adjacent to the supermarket.\n“Following the success of the Plymouth location, the Chanhassen location represents the continued growth of the Coborn’s Market & Table concept,” a company spokesperson said in an email to Bring Me The News on Wednesday.\nRSP Architects\nRSP Architects\nRSP Architects\nRSP Architects\nCurrent plans for Avienda, located across the street from Chanhassen City Hall, call for eight other retail spaces near Coborn’s, including at least two sit-down restaurants and three drive-thru restaurants, and a medical retail building.\nA new community center and a 412-unit apartment building are also being built as part of the development.\nThe Chanhassen Planning Commission will consider the project’s updated site plan, which includes the Coborn’s store, in September.\nThe development team, led by Life Time-affiliated owner Level 7 Development, expects to break ground on the grocery store in the spring and finish ahead of a summer 2028 opening.\nRSP Architects\nRSP Architects\nRSP Architects\nRSP Architects\n1",
    "summary": "Coborn’s is duplicating its new grocery store concept, highlighting local products and chefs within a sprawling mixed-use development in downtown Chanhassen. The second Coborn’s Market & Table location is planned as part of the Avienda project that’s currently underway on a 118-acre site near Highway 212 and Powers Boulevard. Like the prototype in Plymouth that debuted in March , the full-service grocery store will have a dining area with made-to-order and prepackaged food from The Global Table by chef David Fhima and “Cupcake Wars” winner Alicia Hinze’s The Buttered Tin, plus an old-fashioned ice cream parlor and a liquor store.",
